#:layer #!pragmatics

^["roots"]{
 @plan: ~".agents/plans/bias-resolution-product/PLAN.md"
 @normalize: ~"packages/spw-seed/src/normalize.ts"
 @presets: ~"packages/spw-seed/src/query/presets.ts"
 @coupling: ~"packages/spw-seed/src/types/coupling.ts"
 @rel: ~".spw/biome/ocean/expr/rel.spw"   # existing register_bias array convention
 @physics: ~".spw/registries/brace-physics.spw"  # valence pentad markers
}

^["intent"]{
 ~#goal: "Canonicalize `={ target }` as a named resolution-bias product. Today `=`+body normalizes to an inert `config` body with no product identity; give it product `bias` so it is queryable and honored. Frame `[reg=…]` names the axis, body-sequence ranks targets (first strongest), `~`-path is the target form, valence signs direction (boon=attractor, bane=repeller)."
 ~#taste: "expressiveness + naming — a shape the parser already accepts becomes a first-class, addressable product with zero grammar surgery; reuses the existing register_bias ordered-array convention and the ~/valence vocabulary rather than inventing new glyphs"
}

^["development"]{
 lane: #execution_truth
 ladder: #service
 develops: [#taste, #linguistics, #design_transfer]
 review_surfaces: [@plan, @normalize, @rel]
 snippet_routes: [#example, #query, #snippet]
}

^["concept_index"]{
 primary: [#verb_polymorphic_edge, #named_product, #anchor_target_relation]
 surfaces: [#seed, #query, #cli_mount, #cli_mutate, #cli_expand, #canon]
 tensions: [#verb_leak_into_product, #product_vs_boundary_match, #fold_unfold_reversibility]
 transfers: [#language, #mutation_program, #template_lineage, #service]
}

^["parallel_work"]{
 critical_path: [#normalize_product]     # everything downstream keys off the product name
 parallelizable: [#canon_docs, #selector_preset]
 shared_hot_files: ["packages/spw-seed/src/normalize.ts", "packages/spw-seed/src/query/types.ts"]
 merge_risks: [#hot_file, #naming_drift]
 handoff_artifacts: [@plan]
}

^["risk_model"]{
 # hard: product name collides with an existing reg id → downstream selectors mis-hit
 # soft: `product` field absent from SpwPattern → BIAS preset falls back to withBoundaries:['body'] (broader match)
 # non_negotiable: `=`+body that carried NO product before must still parse and normalize identically except for the added product tag (no roundtrip regression)
}

^["validation"]{
 # hypotheses: [ "every current ={…} in the corpus normalizes unchanged except for product:'bias'" ]
 # negative_controls: [ "=[x] frame-only and bare = ops keep reg:'config' with no product", "'.'-facet product untouched" ]
 # demo_path: [ "spw:select a fixture --selector bias → hits the ={~\"...\"} node", "readBias(node) → { axis, targets, sign }" ]
}

^["stream"]{
 >>[2026-07-22 19:10] decide — bias reading chosen over self-ref: self-ref is redundant with filename, collides with ~ (locative), and distributed self-declaration fights .spw/index.spw routing. bias is coherent with =:config and reuses register_bias arrays.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:12] observe — seam is normalize.ts:220; product is stamped only for '.'→facet today. `=`+body-only currently yields reg:'config' with an unnamed body coupling.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:14] observe — SpwPattern (query/types.ts) has no `product` field; BIAS preset can match `{sigil:'=', withBoundaries:['body']}` today but a precise product match needs the field + matcher support.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:15] observe — subject slot is gated to ^ and ? only (expressions.ts:278); `="reg"{…}` subject-binding is the one stretch form that touches the grammar.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:16] taste — target form composes cleanly: = says "lean toward", ~ says "this surface"; nesting keeps locative meaning with ~ and directional meaning with =, dissolving the self-ref tension.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:24] decide — all opens resolved by user: axis slot = frame [reg=depth] (match binding idiom); product id = `bias`; subject-binding IN scope (grammar touch at expressions.ts:278); mount consumption IN scope — spw:mount honors bias resolution, not just portable readBias.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:25] revise — commit sequence grows 6→8: add =-subject grammar commit and a cli-mount consumer commit. readBias stays the portable seam mount calls.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:31] decide — subject slot carries the ANCHOR (from-pole / biased entity), NOT the register name (register is already the frame [reg=…] — subject would be redundant). This makes `=` a binary relation anchor→targets; bare `={ }` = reflexive special case (anchor elided = enclosing node). Directly feeds mount: subject=key, body=target = a routing rule.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:32] taste — three slots earn three distinct jobs: subject=who, frame=along-which-register, body=toward-what. Keep subject monovalent (anchor only) — do NOT overload with scope guards (=(when:…){…}); that ambiguity is deferred to a separate concern.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:41] decide — WIDEN. Product is a neutral verb-polymorphic edge { anchor, axis, targets, sign }; meaning is chosen by the consumer verb, not baked into the product. Ship 3 consumers this branch: mount (resolution), mutate (rewrite; boon=apply/bane=revert; body-sequence = ordered patch), expand (template; unfold=boon, fold=bane — a specialization of mutate against a template edge).
 >>[2026-07-22 19:42] surprise — the rejected self-ref returns transformed: reflexive `={ ~"template" }` (anchor elided) is not location-declaration but PROVENANCE/lineage — "expanded from / conforms to this." The expand consumer reads exactly that.
 >>[2026-07-22 19:43] taste — invariant to protect: normalize/readBias must stay verb-neutral. No consumer word (resolve/rewrite/template) may leak into the seed product shape; seed emits the edge, CLI verbs interpret. This is the whole point of the polymorphism.
 >>[2026-07-23 00:10] implement — commit 1: dedicated `=`+body bias block in normalize.ts. Old body branch was gated !op.frame, so `=[axis]{ target }` (frame+body) dropped its coupling entirely; new block owns all `=`+body, stamps product:'bias' uniformly. Verified: ={x}, =[depth]{x}, =[depth]{deep shallow}, =boon{x} all → product bias; .{} facet untouched. 362 seed tests pass (1 pre-existing isProseCommentLine failure unrelated).
 >>[2026-07-23 00:12] surprise — the confirmed axis spelling `[reg=depth]` DOES NOT PARSE: `=[reg=depth]{…}` collapses to Prose, and so does `^/./!/?[reg=depth]` — the `reg=` binding is not accepted as a direct operator frame on ANY operator (it is a POSTFIX form in real surfaces: `?(args)[reg=buf]`). Bare `=[depth]{ x }` parses cleanly as frame+body. Axis spelling is now an open decision — see open[axis-spelling].
 >>[2026-07-23 00:20] decide — axis spelling = BARE prefix `=[depth]{ target }` (user). reg= redundant inside a bias frame anyway; zero grammar debt; unblocks readBias (4) + grammar (5). readBias reads the axis as the frame's first Parameter identifier.
 >>[2026-07-23 00:30] implement — commits 2-4: query product field + matcher (getNodeProduct mirrors normalize), BIAS preset + `bias` CLI selector (verified `spw:select --selector bias` → 2 edges), readBias → BiasEdge {anchor,axis,targets,sign,valence} verb-neutral (sign forward/inverse from valence, never verb words). 6 unit tests green.
 >>[2026-07-23 00:45] implement — commit 5 (grammar): `=` anchor binding at expressions.ts, gated to OPERATOR-led subjects only (choice(pathRefNode, referenceNode)). Bare identifier stays operatorLabel (=ref/=workbench preserved), string stays config — zero collision, zero refactor needed. Verified =@node→ref anchor, =~"a"→path anchor; ={x}/=[depth]{x}/=boon{x} unchanged. lint:spw 343/343 pass. 8 readBias tests green (added anchor + label-preservation cases).
 >>[2026-07-23 00:46] note — user: "I don't mind needing to refactor." Conservative gate needed none — label/anchor/axis/targets are 4 distinct slots that coexist. Broader anchor form (identifier anchors, reinterpreting =ref/=workbench) remains available if wanted; would refactor ~6 canon usages. Open for discussion before consumers.
 >>[2026-07-23 00:55] surprise — canon's real mount-ref idiom is `=ref: path` (a Binding: key=`=ref` op, value=path), NOT body form. readBias returns null for it → mount consumer would find ZERO real edges. Corpus split: only canon-mount.spw:49-51 are surface-pointing resolution edges; the rest (=dialect:/=warmth:/=probe_window:10/=raw:) are value-config bindings, NOT mount refs.
 >>[2026-07-23 00:57] decide — user chose: refactor canon to body form (one canonical spelling), keep product body-only. Scoped tight to the 3 resolution edges in canon-mount.spw; value-config Bindings left as-is (they hold scalars, not surfaces — not bias edges). commit 6 inserted; consumers shift to 7-9, tests 10, docs 11.
 >>[2026-07-23 01:00] implement — commit 6: canon-mount.spw =ref: <.spw#anchor> → =ref{ ~"<.spw#anchor>" } (x3). `spw:select --selector bias` now finds 3 real edges in canon; lint:spw 343/343 pass; readBias reads full path#anchor targets.
 >>[2026-07-23 01:10] implement — commit 7 (mount consumer): `spw mount resolve [root]` reads each bias edge as a pointer (anchor↦target, sign-aware arrow), verifies surface (path) targets exist, flags dangling, exits 1 on any. Read-only — no --write. Verified on whole .spw tree: 3 edges, 0 dangling, all ✓. Added spw:mount:resolve npm script (base spw:mount hardcodes `check`, so `-- resolve` can't override).
 >>[2026-07-23 01:11] taste — mount reads the edge purely as resolution: follows the pointer, checks existence, never rewrites. First proof the neutral edge carries a verb chosen by the consumer. mutate (8) + expand (9) will read the SAME edge differently.
 >>[2026-07-23 01:25] implement — commit 8 (rewrite consumer): shared bias-apply.ts (biasRewriteRules lifts =@from{ @to } edges → rules in source order; applyBiasRewrites textual, inverse-swaps for bane). mutate `--bias <patch> <targets> [--write]`, PLAN-ONLY unless --write (corpus-wide find/replace = safer default than vanilla mutate). Verified: plan reports hits, file untouched; --write rewrites @Old→@New + path (3 hits); bane patch reverts. Same edge read as rewrite rule.
 >>[2026-07-23 01:26] note — only edges with BOTH anchor+target are rewrites; reflexive/labeled edges (=ref{~"path"}) are skipped by biasRewriteRules — they are resolution/template edges, not rewrites. The consumer's lens decides which edges are in-scope.
 >>[2026-07-23 01:40] implement — commit 9 (template consumer): new `spw expand` command. Reflexive path edge ={ ~"tpl" } = provenance. unfold (boon) materializes template between # >>expand / # <<expand markers; fold (bane) strips them. Plan-only unless --write. Verified ROUNDTRIP IDENTITY (unfold→fold = original). Registered in aggregate router (run.ts) + spw:expand script + scripts/spw-expand.ts shim. Fixed template path resolution (workspace-root first, then surface dir).
 >>[2026-07-23 01:42] taste — POLYMORPHISM PROVEN: one neutral edge, three consumers, three verbs — mount(resolution: anchor↦target+existence), mutate(rewrite: from→to, boon/bane), expand(template: unfold/fold, reversible). Each reads the SAME readBias edge; the verb lives in the consumer, never in seed. The widen was worth it.
 >>[2026-07-23 02:05] surprise — probed idiomatic boundaries for expand: `(expand: …)` scope and `<< … >>@ref` stream both parse, but ANY multiline foreign content (^seed[…] etc.) breaks every in-file boundary into Prose. The marker was never the problem — INLINING a foreign surface into the source can't round-trip the parser. Boundary choice can't fix that.
 >>[2026-07-23 02:07] decide — refine expand → PROJECTION, not in-file mutation. The edge IS the canonical fold; source is never rewritten. `spw expand` renders the unfolded view (stdout / derived .expanded.spw), each template framed in a `<< … >>` stream (idiomatic flow boundary) with a provenance line, recursive + cycle-guarded + depth-bounded. Kills the whole brittleness class (markers, re-parse, reversibility). Better AST use = project the tree, don't text-surgery it.
 >>[2026-07-23 02:12] implement — dedup pass (user): extracted bias-edges.ts (biasSites + resolveTilde) — the ONE place the CLI reads edges / resolves tilde paths. mount, mutate(bias-apply), expand all consume it; removed triplicated fromSource+readBias loops and the double-exists path heuristic. Verified: mount 3 edges unchanged, mutate rule unchanged, expand recursive+cycle-guarded, cli 94 pass, lint 343/343, typecheck baseline.
 >>[2026-07-23 02:13] note — expand no longer has fold/unfold: projection means the source is always folded, so fold is definitional and --fold is meaningless. boon/bane stays meaningful for mutate (apply/revert); expand just projects. Not every consumer uses every edge dimension.
 >>[2026-07-23 00:32] surprise — TWO seed trees. `packages/spw-seed/src/` is the REAL code; `src/seed/*.ts` are re-export shims (`export * from ../../packages/...`). test:seed globs ONLY `src/seed/**/*.test.ts` — the `packages/spw-seed/src/**/*.test.ts` files (e.g. form-sequence.test.ts) are ORPHANED, run by no config. Package tests must live in `src/seed/__tests__/` and import via `@spwashi/spw-seed`. Relocated read-bias.test.ts there; it runs. Saved to memory.
}

